<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyrus the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 600–<a href="/years/530-bce">530 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Cyrus II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyrus the Great, or Cyrus II, was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, who acceded in 559 BCE as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a>. He overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a> and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>. His policy of absorbing existing political and religious traditions, including Elamite ones, became a model for Persian rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great.jpg" alt="Cyrus the Great" width="261" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Surenae · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrus_II_(The_Great)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cyrus came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid dynasty</a> took its name from Achaemenes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> traced his ancestry to him. Achaemenes was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teispes">Teispes</a>, who took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">King of Anshan</a> after seizing the city of Anshan, and Teispes had a son, Cyrus I. Cyrus II was born to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-i">Cambyses I</a>, King of Anshan, and Mandane, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a>, King of Media, in about 600-599 BCE. Herodotus gave a mythological account of his youth: Astyages, warned by dreams that his grandson would supplant him, ordered the child killed, but a shepherd raised him instead. When Cyrus succeeded his father in 559 BCE he still had to recognise Median overlordship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cyrus overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a> in about 550 BCE. He then conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> had captured the city of Pteria in Cappadocia; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pteria">Battle of Pteria</a> ended in a stalemate and Croesus withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. The exact date of the Lydian conquest is unknown, and the once common date of 547 BCE is no longer widely held. Between about 545 and 540 BCE Cyrus campaigned in the east, where an attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedrosia">Gedrosia</a> failed but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, Sogdia, Chorasmia and other provinces were taken. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle">Nabonidus Chronicle</a> shows his title changing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">King of Anshan</a> to King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><p>By 540 BCE he had captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and its capital, Susa. Shortly before October 539 BCE he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-opis">Battle of Opis</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and routed the army of the Neo-Babylonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>. On 10 October Sippar was seized without a battle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a>, a clay cylinder in Akkadian cuneiform placed in the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a>, the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> at Babylon, portrays Cyrus as pleasing to Marduk and describes how he repatriated displaced peoples and restored temples. The return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a> has been read as part of this policy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> honours him for it. He divided his empire among capitals at Pasargadae, Babylon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, governed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Accounts of his death differ. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says he died in battle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a>, ruled by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomyris">Tomyris</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> places his death in fighting with the Derbices; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> says he returned to Pasargadae. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>. A limestone tomb at Pasargadae, built around 540-530 BCE, is believed by many to be his; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> gives its epitaph as an address by Cyrus, who won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> their empire.</p><p>Greek writers noted his tolerance of the customs and religions of conquered peoples, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> says his subjects called him their Father. Pierre Briant holds that too little is known to reconstruct his own religion. Later Persian empires often looked on the Achaemenid era as an ideal to emulate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cyrus the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Achaemenid Empire, the first Persian Empire, was founded by <b>Cyrus II</b> (Cyrus the Great) after he became King of Anshan in Persia around 559 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Cyrus the Great</b>'s Persian forces defeated the Babylonian army at Opis in the autumn of 539 BCE, Babylon itself fell without meaningful resistance in October; the Marduk priesthood appears to have welcomed Cyrus, and the Cyrus Cylinder presented the conquest as a divinely ordained restoration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Cyrus the Great</b> conquered Lydia in 546 BCE, Chios, according to Herodotus, submitted to the Achaemenid Empire of its own accord, although the Persians as yet had no fleet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Cyrus the Great</b> took Babylon in 539 BCE Dilmun passed to the Achaemenid Empire, and it disappears from the record as a political entity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cyrus the Great</b> absorbed Elam into the Achaemenid Empire in the 540s and 530s BCE and ended its political independence, yet Darius I soon made Susa a principal Persian royal capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Cyrus the Great</b> defeated Croesus in 546 BCE, Persian generals reduced the Ionian cities one by one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Anshan Persia, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.01,52.41&amp;year=-555" aria-label="Anshan Persia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5912 1068 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cyrus the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6362 1338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6362 1338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.01,52.41&amp;year=-555">What was here in 555 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Tell Astyages that I shall appear in his presence sooner than he will like.&#8221;</p><footer>Herodotus, Histories, book 1 &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus' account &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_1" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…soft countries gave birth to soft men - there was no region which produced very delightful fruits, and at the same time men of a warlike spirit.&#8221;</p><footer>Herodotus, Histories, book 9 &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus' account of his answer to the Persians &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_9" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The LORD God of heaven hath given me all the kingdoms of the earth; and he hath charged me to build him an house at Jerusalem, which is in Judah.&#8221;</p><footer>Book of Ezra 1:2 &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; his decree as the Book of Ezra gives it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Ezra" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Vice, passing lightly along her path of pleasure, wins the hearts of thousands with her gifts; but Virtue, toiling up the steep ascent, has little skill to snare the souls of men and draw them after her…&#8221;</p><footer>Xenophon, Cyropaedia, book 2 &#183; tr. H. G. Dakyns, 1914 &#183; Xenophon's account, in his idealised life of Cyrus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2085/pg2085-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This my fatherland, which was once of no account in Asia, I leave at the height of power, and of all that I won I think I have lost nothing.&#8221;</p><footer>Xenophon, Cyropaedia, book 8 (on his deathbed) &#183; tr. H. G. Dakyns, 1914 &#183; Xenophon's account, in his idealised life of Cyrus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2085/pg2085-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have never yet been afraid of any men, who have a set place in the middle of their city, where they come together to cheat each other and forswear themselves.&#8221;</p><footer>To a Spartan herald who warned him not to harm the Greek cities of Asia (Herodotus, Histories, book 1) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account, who says he meant the market-places of the Greeks, unknown to the Persians &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_1" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
